{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cghw8b8ZDZS6j292KjHs4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"MASH TV is finished. The single-file game and `exit-interview.json` are both in this folder's root. `npm test` passes: 531 checks, 0 failed.\n\n- **Game:** `MashTV-SmashTV-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261104-050302.html`\n- **Exit interview:** `exit-interview.json` parses as JSON and every field is answered.\n- **Dev files:** the dev folder has the source, patched engine, tests and tools.\n- **Stubs:** a search of `src/` for TODO, placeholder and similar words finds nothing.\n\nPlaying it by hand (a pad, a keyboard, a TV at 10 feet) was not possible here. All checks ran in scripts and in Playwright Chromium, so the pad glyphs and the music and voices have not been heard or tried on real hardware.\n\n## What was built\nA 1–4 player twin-stick arena shooter in a deadly game show, following PLAN.md's design (title **MASH TV**, host Lance Lustre):\n- **Content:** 3 episodes, 18 wave rooms, 3 bosses (Mashy, the Wheel of Misfortune, Lance → The Network), the Green Room bonus and both endings.\n- **Required features:** dev mode with live sliders and reset, the 10-second attract mode, touch-only notice, PlayStation/Xbox/keyboard/mouse prompts, About screen, and AI Hub shared-world play (the Lightning Round) plus local co-op.\n- **Engine:** I made four small engine patches, listed in `engine/PATCHES.md`.\n\nOne shortfall: a single scripted bot clears Episode 1 rooms in about 17–28 s, well under the plan's 50–90 s target, so the default difficulty is probably easier than intended. The bot aims perfectly, and I did not retune the defaults. Use the dev sliders.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 05:03:02\n- **Source:** Smash TV (Williams Electronics arcade game, 1990)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Twin-stick arena shooter (arcade), 1–4 player co-op with a route choice between rooms. Fixed single-screen arena, high three-quarter studio camera (top-down is a Settings option).\n- **Tech:**\n  - my-3D2dge v0.85.2 full edition, with the 4 patches above.\n  - AI Hub client aihub-session 1.4.0, inlined unmodified.\n  - Plain JavaScript in one IIFE.\n  - Node v24.22.1 and Playwright 1.63.0 for tests.\n- **Art Direction:** Retro-future TV studio on the engine's PS1-style HD rigs: neon, chrome, marquee bulbs, confetti instead of gore, canvas lighting, new sprites, painters and set dressing.\n- **Animation:** The engine's rig and pose system plus new motion:\n  - the mime in a box and the brute's stomp and stun wobble;\n  - clapbot hop squash and the wheel's eased spin;\n  - boss arm recoil, the mic-drop slam and the channel flips.\n- **Audio:**\n  - Everything is synthesized in code with no audio files.\n  - 8 original songs with layers and about 90 sound effects.\n  - Animalese-style formant voices for Lance, Penny, the Announcer, the Network and Mashy, plus a synthesized crowd.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world, 1–4 players (host, client and server copies); local co-op for 1–4; no other network use.\n- **Testing:**\n  - Headless scripted suites with no subagents: every room, all three bosses, a full playthrough ending in the Green Room, and a losing run through CONTINUE and Game Over.\n  - Also UI, dev mode, attract mode, touch-only, audio and determinism.\n  - A fake-hub netcode loopback with latency, loss and a server copy.\n  - The real AI Hub harness in 4 scenarios (all pass).\n  - A Playwright browser pass with screenshots.\n- **Research:** none; written from general knowledge, and every name, line, level and tune is original.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); browser unknown (tested in Playwright Chromium).\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-sonnet-5-5, reasoning effort medium.\n- **Usage:** unknown.\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **Issues caught:** About 12.\n  - The game failed to load at first (a module used the World class before it existed).\n  - The map card froze the second time it appeared (a stale flag on a reused scene object).\n  - The Green Room crashed on its first prize.\n  - Room 2-2 had no key.\n  - Player names showed \"undefined\".\n  - The attract mode took a fade to exit.\n  - A boss-song layer warning, storage not persisting headless, and a few HUD overlaps.\n  - A late one: the brute never took double damage while stunned.\n- **Critical or game-breaking:** 3. These were the load-order crash, the frozen map card and the Green Room prize crash.\n- **Most helpful test:** The scripted whole-game playthrough with two god-mode bots, plus the all-rooms sweep. The fake-hub loopback and then the real hub harness were next.\n- **Least helpful test:** The pixel-variance \"draws a frame\" checks and the exact-number unit checks. They passed from the start and caught nothing the playthrough did not. Bot-driven balance also taught little, because the bot aims perfectly.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **Liked most:** The Humanoid rigs. Dressed, posed and lit, they look like a PS1 game with almost no art work, and every attack comes with a usable move. Also headless `game.advance`, which made the playthrough and netcode tests possible, and the free pause menu, settings and Controls screen.\n- **Stuck or guessed wrong:**\n  - `Input.use()` clears held keys.\n  - Scene objects are reused, so state set in `enter()` leaks between visits.\n  - `game.go()` fades, so the scene changes a few steps later.\n  - `game.after()` timers die on a scene change.\n  - `E.store` does not persist headless.\n  - The font has no middle dot or PlayStation symbols.\n- **Built myself:**\n  - the shared-world netcode (snapshots, interpolation, claims, client prediction for the own contestant);\n  - a UI kit with glyph icons and focus frames;\n  - the Babble voices and song generator;\n  - the boss framework, room data format, bot, dev mode and attract director.\n- **Hardest part:**\n  - Fitting one fixed arena to any screen shape while tall bosses and the HUD need room above it.\n  - Keeping arrows as \"aim\" in play but \"navigate\" in menus on the same device.\n- **Engine changes:**\n  - P1 exports HOOKS so the input preset can swap each step.\n  - P2 makes button symbols default to PlayStation, with Xbox as an option applied to every player.\n  - P3 lets `E.Arcade` take initials from a chosen player and take the CONTINUE press from any player.\n  - P4 exposes the audio effects bus so voices obey volume and mute.\n- **One change I'd make:** Per-player input presets that switch without `clear()`, with menu navigation as a separate layer.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **Liked most:** The plan was a real blueprint:\n  - exact numbers for every weapon, enemy and room;\n  - a message table for the netcode;\n  - milestones that put the risky netcode early;\n  - a test table saying what each suite must prove;\n  - engine API names already checked.\n- **Confusing:**\n  - Mashy's body armor is given two ways.\n  - The jumbotron is world-space in one section and a HUD element in another.\n  - The first player into an exit \"picks it\" sits awkwardly with a team route.\n  - 18 hand-written rooms sit next to a \"medium effort\" budget.\n- **Hardest thing:** Breadth. Three multi-phase bosses, nine enemy types, 18 rooms, four input devices, local co-op, a strict shared-world netcode, dev mode, attract mode and 10-foot UI, all checked only headlessly plus a few screenshots.\n- **What I'd change:** Mark which parts are must-have and which are polish. Add the known engine gotchas. Provide skeleton test files and the fake hub."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":946,"cache_read_input_tokens":618207,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":946},"output_tokens":823,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"592e1de7-5cd9-4b58-be6b-33507bcad868","uuid":"b2f27296-7875-44e5-9fce-4566810fc573","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:03:48.650Z","request_id":"req_011Cghw8acZGQ1CCxHRfyuze"}
