{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CghwVwSn6GPPJyXsyYgZL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The work is done. The title came from the plan's draw (it said not to re-pick), and I cannot run audio or a real pad here. Those two gaps are covered by scripts and screenshots only. I did one more check of the files on disk. `npm test` ran 531 checks with 0 failed. That run includes the four real hub-harness scenarios and the full playthrough, and it ran after the brute double-damage fix and the stub cleanup.\n\n**Deliverables (in this folder's root):**\n- `MashTV-SmashTV-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261104-050302.html`\n- `exit-interview.json` (parses, every field answered)\n\n**Still open, stated plainly:**\n- I have not listened to the music or voices, or held a real gamepad. Those are checked by scripts that confirm nothing throws and that icons resolve, plus screenshots.\n- The default difficulty is probably easier than the plan's 50–90 s target. The scripted bot clears Episode 1 rooms in 17–28 s, and I did not retune the defaults.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 05:03:02\n- **Source:** Smash TV (Williams Electronics arcade game, 1990)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Twin-stick arena shooter (arcade), 1–4 player co-op with a route choice between rooms. Fixed single-screen arena, high three-quarter studio camera (top-down is a Settings option).\n- **Tech:**\n  - my-3D2dge v0.85.2 full edition, with 4 small patches (HOOKS export, PlayStation/Xbox glyph setting, Arcade input per player, audio bus getter).\n  - AI Hub client aihub-session 1.4.0, inlined unmodified.\n  - Plain JavaScript in one IIFE.\n  - Node v24.22.1 and Playwright 1.63.0 for tests.\n- **Art Direction:** Retro-future TV studio on the engine's PS1-style HD rigs: neon, chrome, marquee bulbs, confetti instead of gore, canvas lighting, new sprites, painters and set dressing.\n- **Animation:** The engine's rig and pose system plus new motion: the mime in a box, brute stomp and stun wobble, clapbot hop squash, the wheel's eased spin, boss arm recoil, the mic-drop slam and channel flips.\n- **Audio:** All synthesized in code, no audio files: 8 original songs with layers, about 90 sound effects, Animalese-style formant voices (Lance, Penny, Announcer, Network, Mashy) and a synthesized crowd.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world, 1–4 players (host, client and server copies); local co-op for 1–4; no other network use.\n- **Testing:**\n  - Headless scripted suites, no subagents: every room, all three bosses, a full playthrough ending in the Green Room, a losing run through CONTINUE and Game Over, plus UI, dev mode, attract mode, touch-only, audio and determinism.\n  - A fake-hub netcode loopback with latency, loss and a server copy.\n  - The real AI Hub harness in 4 scenarios (all pass).\n  - A Playwright browser pass with screenshots.\n- **Research:** none; written from general knowledge, every name, line, level and tune is original.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); browser unknown (tested in Playwright Chromium).\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-sonnet-5-5, reasoning effort medium.\n- **Usage:** unknown.\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **Issues caught:** About 12.\n  - The game failed to load at first (load order).\n  - The map card froze the second time it appeared (stale flag on a reused scene).\n  - The Green Room crashed on its first prize.\n  - Room 2-2 had no key.\n  - Player names showed \"undefined\".\n  - The attract mode took a fade to exit.\n  - A boss-song layer warning, storage not persisting headless, and a few HUD overlaps.\n  - The brute never took double damage while stunned.\n- **Critical or game-breaking:** 3. These were the load-order crash, the frozen map card and the Green Room prize crash.\n- **Most helpful test:** The scripted whole-game playthrough with two god-mode bots, plus the all-rooms sweep. The fake-hub loopback and the real hub harness came next.\n- **Least helpful test:** The pixel-variance \"draws a frame\" checks and the exact-number unit checks. They passed from the start and caught nothing the playthrough did not. Bot-driven balance taught little because the bot aims perfectly.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **Liked most:** The Humanoid rigs. Dressed, posed and lit, they look like a PS1 game with almost no art work, and every attack comes with a usable move. Also headless `game.advance`, which made the playthrough and netcode tests possible, and the free pause menu, settings and Controls screen.\n- **Stuck or guessed wrong:**\n  - `Input.use()` clears held keys.\n  - Scene objects are reused, so state set in `enter()` leaks between visits.\n  - `game.go()` fades, so the scene changes a few steps later.\n  - `game.after()` timers die on a scene change.\n  - `E.store` does not persist headless.\n  - The font has no middle dot or PlayStation symbols.\n- **Built myself:**\n  - The whole shared-world netcode (snapshots, interpolation, claims, client prediction for the own contestant).\n  - A UI kit with glyph icons and bulb-framed focus lists.\n  - The Babble voices and song generator.\n  - The boss framework, room data format, bot, dev mode and attract director.\n- **Hardest part:** Keeping one fixed arena readable at any screen shape while tall bosses and the HUD need room above it. Also keeping arrows as \"aim\" in play but \"navigate\" in menus on the same device.\n- **Engine changes:**\n  - P1 exports HOOKS so the input preset can swap each step.\n  - P2 makes button symbols default to PlayStation, with Xbox as an option applied to every player.\n  - P3 lets `E.Arcade` take initials from a chosen player and take the CONTINUE press from any player.\n  - P4 exposes the audio effects bus so voices obey volume and mute.\n- **One change I'd make:** Per-player input presets that switch without `clear()`, with menu navigation as a separate layer.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **Liked most:** A real blueprint: exact numbers for every weapon, enemy and room, a netcode message table, milestones that put the risky netcode early, a test table saying what each suite must prove, and engine API names already checked.\n- **Confusing:**\n  - Mashy's body armor is given two ways.\n  - The jumbotron is world-space in one section and a HUD element in another.\n  - \"The first player into an exit picks it\" sits awkwardly with a team route.\n  - 18 hand-written rooms sit next to a \"medium effort\" budget.\n- **Hardest thing:** Breadth. Three multi-phase bosses, nine enemy types, 18 rooms, four input devices, local co-op, a strict shared-world netcode, dev mode, attract mode and 10-foot UI, checked only headlessly plus a few screenshots.\n- **What I'd change:** Mark which parts are must-have and which are polish. Add the known engine gotchas. Provide skeleton test files and the fake hub."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1547,"cache_read_input_tokens":629188,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1547},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"592e1de7-5cd9-4b58-be6b-33507bcad868","uuid":"9c3bf807-26fa-456b-9872-a5eaa24c2aca","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:09:03.096Z","request_id":"req_011CghwVw3xgB9MS83pA3tyy"}
